DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The xxoo__eemmiitt() function emits formatted output using the description in a
     format string along with a set of zero or more arguments, in a style sim‐
     ilar to printf(3) but using a more complex format description string, as
     described in xo_format(5).

     xxoo__eemmiitt() uses the default output handle, as described in libxo(3), where
     xxoo__eemmiitt__hh() uses an explicit handle.  xxoo__eemmiitt__hhvv() accepts a _v_a___l_i_s_t for
     additional flexibility.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     In this example, a set of four values is emitted using the following
     source code:

               xo_emit(" {:lines/%7ju} {:words/%7ju} "
                       "{:characters/%7ju} {d:filename/%s}\n",
                       linect, wordct, charct, file);
     Output can then be generated in various style, using the "--libxo" op‐
     tion:

               % wc /etc/motd
                     25     165    1140 /etc/motd
               % wc --libxo xml,pretty,warn /etc/motd
               <wc>
                 <file>
                   <lines>25</lines>
                   <words>165</words>
                   <characters>1140</characters>
                   <filename>/etc/motd</filename>
                 </file>
               </wc>
               % wc --libxo json,pretty,warn /etc/motd
               {
                 "wc": {
                   "file": [
                     {
                       "lines": 25,
                       "words": 165,
                       "characters": 1140,
                       "filename": "/etc/motd"
                     }
                   ]
                 }
               }
               % wc --libxo html,pretty,warn /etc/motd
               <div class="line">
                 <div class="text"> </div>
                 <div class="data" data-tag="lines">     25</div>
                 <div class="text"> </div>
                 <div class="data" data-tag="words">    165</div>
                 <div class="text"> </div>
                 <div class="data" data-tag="characters">   1140</div>
                 <div class="text"> </div>
                 <div class="data" data-tag="filename">/etc/motd</div>
               </div>

RREETTUURRNN CCOODDEE
     xxoo__eemmiitt returns a negative value on error.  If the XXOOFF__CCOOLLUUMMNNSS flag has
     been turned on for the specific handle using xo_set_flags(3), then the
     number of display columns consumed by the output will be returned.

